  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in feet) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=3{e}^{-t}+3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t-3t,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{e}^{-t}+3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t-3t) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-t}+\\frac{3}{t}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=3,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =-3{e}^{-3}+\\frac{3}{3}-3 \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3}-2 \\\\  & \u2248-2.15\\,\\text{ft/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-2.15|=2.15\\,\\text{ft/s}.$",
